      {
        "review_id": 366482367,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r366482367-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Shame on Nepal",
        "text": "We returned from Nepal a month ago.  First visit since the earthquake one year ago this week, but my 5th or 6th visit since 1967.  Pashipatinath used to be a fun place to visit.  It, and Muktinath, are two of the 51 Shakti Pithas revered by Hindus, that are found in the hills of Nepal.  Located on the bank of the Bhagmati River, and now a World Heritage site, it is a 20 minute taxi ride from the center of Kathmandu, and just west of the airport.  In 1967, there were open fields and rice paddys around the temple, but now there is solid suburban sprawl.  And, as before, only \"Hindus\" are allowed into the temple.  Us heathen non-Hindus can only peer into the entrance around the giant Nandi, or look down from across the river, which in itself is entertaining, but not satisfying.  I say shame on Nepal because this sacred river is now a sewer filled with trash and plastic bottles.  Why they would allow this to happen to a World Heritage site is beyond me.  The universal attitude there, and many other areas in Nepal, is \"It's not my job.\"  Nepal has received millions of dollars in foreign aid, but the government and the overseers of the temple can't even organize a trash pickup.  For a country that earns most of it's foreign exchange from tourism and mountaineering, revers a sacred river in its capital, and has hoards of destitute citizens looking for work, why they can't accomplish such a simple, low-tech task like picking up trash, is beyond me.\n    Instead of Pashupatti, I suggest going to the nearby Baudanath Stupa.  It too suffered damage from the earthquake, but repairs are well underway.  Visitors can circumambulate the stupa and unlike Pashuaptti, are welcome to enter the numerous Tibetan Gompas surrounding the stupa.  There are several places to get lunch or tea, and a nice selection of shops.  Best of all, there are no cars or motorcycles allowed in the walkway around the stupa.  Highly recommended.",
